  "question": {
    "text": "If $\\int \\frac{\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{x}}(1+\\sin \\mathrm{x}) \\mathrm{dx}}{1+\\cos \\mathrm{x}}=\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})+\\mathrm{C}$, then $\\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})$ is equal to",
    "image": null
  },
  "options": [
    {
      "id": "KgAbd",
      "text": "$\\sin \\frac{x}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "lalXq",
      "text": "$\\cos \\frac{x}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    },
    {
      "id": "sOInl",
      "text": "$\\tan \\frac{x}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": true
    },
    {
      "id": "K5AGC",
      "text": "$\\log \\frac{x}{2}$",
      "image": null,
      "isCorrect": false
    }
  ],
  "correctValue": null,
  "solution": {
    "text": "$\\int e^x \\frac{(1+\\sin x)}{(1+\\cos x)} d x$<br />$=\\int e^x\\left[\\frac{1}{2} \\sec ^2 \\frac{x}{2}+\\tan \\frac{x}{2}\\right] d x$<br />$=\\frac{1}{2} \\int e^x \\sec ^2 \\frac{x}{2} d x+\\int e^x \\tan \\frac{x}{2} d x$<br />$=\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\tan \\frac{\\mathrm{x}}{2}+\\mathrm{C}$<br />But $\\mathrm{I}=\\mathrm{e}^{\\mathrm{x}} \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})+\\mathrm{C}$ (given)<br />$\\therefore \\mathrm{f}(\\mathrm{x})=\\tan \\frac{\\mathrm{x}}{2}$",
    "image": null
  },
